{"conversation":"topic-switch","question":"Tell me about radar","reply":{"answer":"Pulse-Doppler radar measures range by timing and closure by Doppler shift, using the shift to reject clutter and track targets in velocity [1, 5]. Pulse repetition frequency (PRF) determines unambiguous range and velocity measurement capabilities, with higher PRFs providing excellent velocity resolution but causing range ambiguity [6]. Additionally, a radar warning receiver (RWR) detects radar signals to show a threat's type, mode, and rough relative bearing [3, 5].","ids":["/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar","/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-warning-receiver","/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#recap","/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#prf"],"topic":[],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar","title":"Pulse-Doppler radar"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-warning-receiver","title":"Radar warning receiver"},{"id":"/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#recap","title":"Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures · Key points"},{"id":"/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#prf","title":"Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures · PRF and its trade-offs"}]},"ms":2974,"passed":true,"revision":"4a624fd75a4bc64720c9780a5854e832cd75ebde"}
{"conversation":"comparison","question":"What tower frequencies do Sochi and Kutaisi use?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Caucasus, Sochi-Adler uses VHF 127.0, UHF 256.0, low VHF 39.6, and HF 4.05, while Kutaisi uses VHF 134.0, UHF 263.0, low VHF 41.0, and HF 4.4.","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-kutaisi"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/","/encyclopedia/locations/kutaisi/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","title":"Sochi-Adler · DCS: Caucasus"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-kutaisi","title":"Kutaisi · DCS: Caucasus"}]},"ms":2032,"passed":true,"revision":"4a624fd75a4bc64720c9780a5854e832cd75ebde"}
{"conversation":"comparison","question":"Compare the engines of the F-14 and F-16.","reply":{"answer":"The F-14 Tomcat uses two afterburning turbofans, either Pratt & Whitney TF30-P-414A (20,900 lb each) for the F-14A or General Electric F110-GE-400 (27,000 lb each) for the F-14B/D [1, 2]. In contrast, the F-16 Fighting Falcon is a single-engine aircraft using either a General Electric F110-GE-129 (29,500 lbf) on the Block 50 or a Pratt & Whitney F100-PW-229 on the Block 52 [3].","ids":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/#swing-wings-and-phoenix-missiles","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","title":"F-14 Tomcat"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/#swing-wings-and-phoenix-missiles","title":"F-14 Tomcat · Swing wings and Phoenix missiles"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/","title":"F-16 Fighting Falcon"}]},"ms":3532,"passed":true,"revision":"4a624fd75a4bc64720c9780a5854e832cd75ebde"}
